1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:04,000
Biodiversity Loss: The Sixth Mass Extinction.

2
00:00:03,950 --> 00:00:15,662
The Earth is experiencing its sixth mass extinction event, and unlike previous mass extinctions — driven by asteroid impacts or volcanic activity — this one is caused primarily by human activity.

3
00:00:15,662 --> 00:00:28,875
The 2019 IPBES Global Assessment Report, drawing on over 15,000 scientific sources, concluded that approximately one million animal and plant species are now threatened with extinction, many within decades.

4
00:00:28,875 --> 00:00:36,025
The current extinction rate is estimated to be tens to hundreds of times higher than the average over the past 10 million years.

5
00:00:36,025 --> 00:00:38,712
The drivers are well documented.

6
00:00:38,712 --> 00:00:46,687
Land-use change — primarily agriculture and urbanisation — has altered over 75% of the Earth's land surface.

7
00:00:46,687 --> 00:00:52,487
Over 85% of wetlands present in 1700 had been lost by 2000.

8
00:00:52,487 --> 00:00:59,037
Direct exploitation of organisms, including overfishing and logging, threatens numerous species.

9
00:00:59,037 --> 00:01:07,987
The IUCN Red List reports that over 41,000 species are currently threatened, representing 28% of all assessed species.

10
00:01:07,987 --> 00:01:11,087
Climate change compounds these pressures.

11
00:01:11,087 --> 00:01:22,262
A 1.5°C to 2°C rise in global temperature is projected to reduce the geographical range of most plant and vertebrate species by 50% or more.

12
00:01:22,262 --> 00:01:30,437
Coral reefs, hosting at least 25% of marine biodiversity, have already experienced widespread bleaching events.

13
00:01:30,437 --> 00:01:40,575
The IPBES report emphasises that transformative changes across food production, energy consumption, and economic systems are required to halt biodiversity decline.

